  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed dwelling presents as an undesirable addition which will alter the unique and symmetrical built-form of Hardie Road and the surrounding residential streets. The proposed dwelling will alter the built-form of the semi-detached pair into a terrace, altering the symmetry of the dwellings having a negative impact upon the street scene. The design of the proposed dwelling does not discordant correlation with the existing dwelling and does not have a unique distinction. Its mass and bulk makes it appear as an extension rather than a separate unit. The proposed dwelling compromises the openness of the corner plot and result in a loss of the sense of spaciousness and green space currently afforded to the site. The proposal would therefore be detrimental to the character and appearance of the surrounding area. It is therefore considered to be at odds with the following policies which aim to ensure that developments are of a high-quality:National Planning Policy Framework National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (December, 2023)Policies D4 and HC1 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies CP2 and CP3 of the Local Development Framework (LDF) Core Strategy (July 2010)Policies BP2, BP8 and BP11 of the Local Development Framework (LDF) Borough Wide Development Plan Document (DPD) (March 2011)The Residential Extensions and Alterations Supplementary Planning Document (February 2012)Policies SP2, DMD4 and DMD6 of The London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Draft Local Plan: (Regulation 19 Submission Version, December 2021)"
  ],
